  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing the Frontend**\n\nI\'ve successfully created the API route, which is a great starting point. Now, I\'m shifting focus to building the main frontend page. I\'m revisiting the initial file list and the `package.json` to understand the base structure, with a focus on `/app/layout.tsx` and `/app/globals.css`.\n\n\n**Identifying Missing Files**\n\nI\'ve realized that `/app/page.tsx` is missing from the initial file list and it requires creation. I\'m planning to make it a component-rich Client Component (with \'use client\') because it handles speech recognition, tracking states, timers, and local storage. Now I need to design it.\n\n\n**Designing the UI**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the UI design, focusing on a robust implementation of the Web Speech API. I\'ve figured out how to safely access `window.SpeechRecognition` in a Next.js SSR environment, preventing potential hydration errors with a null check. Now I\'m meticulously designing the two-column layout, including the sidebar with the title, and the speaker list, considering the \"\353\214\200\353\252\250\354\202\260 \355\230\270\355\205\224 \354\202\254\354\227\205\353\266\200\" corporate style. I also plan to have a color picker for the speakers.\n\n\n**Developing Interactive Features**\n\nI\'m now implementing dynamic speaker management. I\'ll add the ability to delete or append speakers easily. I\'m also focused on the \'\355\232\214\354\235\230 \354\235\264\353\240\245 \353\252\251\353\241\235\' functionality, loading saved meetings from local storage, allowing them to be loaded with a click, and providing a delete option. For the main content, I\'m designing the top header with the meeting title input, a browser compatibility check, microphone lock status, and a recording state indicator with a visual, interactive badge. Lastly, I\'m working on a quick speaker selector with color-coded pills.\n\n\n**Implementing Core Features**\n\nI\'m now integrating the dynamic speaker tagging based on the currently selected speaker, as per the design document. I\'m focusing on the \"Quick Selector\" to allow for quick assignment. I\'m also preparing the controls box, including start, pause, and stop/save functions, with appropriate icons. I\'m designing the central timeline layout, with a placeholder for the empty state.\n\n\n**Refining the Timeline**\n\nI\'m now honing the timeline feature, focusing on the visual and interactive aspects. I\'m implementing a vertical timeline path using the `.timeline-glow` helper style from `globals.css` and adding staggered entry animations with `motion/react`. Each timeline block will include a timestamp (using the absolute time format), a speaker selector dropdown (matching custom speaker names and states), an editable textarea for the text body that updates the timeline state on change, and a delete button (trash icon) for removing rows. I\'m also planning a clean ambient indicator for the bottom real-time preview, which shows the interim result while the user is speaking.\n\n\n**Adding AI Summary Features**\n\nI\'m now incorporating AI-powered summarization, including a premium tab or drawer to display results. I\'m focusing on a \"\354\232\224\354\225\275 \354\203\235\354\204\261\" button that triggers a server-side API call to `/api/gemini/summarize` with a reassuring loading status. I intend to render the markdown output beautifully with a customized renderer or CSS-styled elements. I will also incorporate export options and a reset feature. I\'m designing the interface elements. I\'m now making them presentable.\n\n\n**Designing the Hook**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the core speech recognition logic, including crafting comprehensive state variables. I\'m structuring a robust hook and designing detailed interfaces for attendees, timeline items, and saved meetings. I need to handle a continuously restarting speech recognition with a custom effect to ensure UX. I\'m also planning to utilize a warning message to guide the user. I\'ll need to use icons from `lucide-react`, such as `Mic`, `MicOff`, `Pause`, `Play`, etc.\n\n\n**Implementing Continuous Listening**\n\nI\'m now focusing on maintaining continuous speech recognition using `recognitionRef.current.onend`. I\'m using `useRef` for `isRecording` to avoid stale closures, per professional React development best practices. I\'m also ensuring the API restarts automatically after a brief silence to maintain continuity. I\'ve designed the logic, as per the `gemini-api` guidelines.\n\n\n**Refining Final Transcripts**\n\nI\'m now implementing the handling of final transcript blocks, specifically appending new `TimelineItem` segments. I\'ve focused on `recognitionRef.current.onresult`, using `event.resultIndex` to manage index-based appending of finals. I\'m extracting and committing final transcripts, and I am also refining the `addTimelineItem` function.\n\n\n**Updating the Timeline**\n\nI\'m now integrating the generation of the timeline items, including both absolute and relative timestamps. I\'m focusing on the `setTranscript` function to append new entries. The integration is robust, especially with the use of refs to prevent stale values, such as the selected speaker.\n\n\n**Implementing Silence Alert**\n\nI\'m now implementing the 30-second silence warning. I\'ll track the last detected speech time and use a `useEffect` interval to check for prolonged silence. If idle for 30 seconds or more, I\'ll display an alert badge and message to notify the user and maintain recording continuity. This feature will prevent endless API calls.\n\n\n**Mapping Attendee Colors**\n\nI\'m now integrating attendee color mapping for enhanced visual distinction within the timeline. I\'ve defined a palette of Tailwind-compatible colors \342\200\223 blue, green, purple, pink, amber, and cyan \342\200\223 to ensure a clean and cohesive user interface, mapping them with corresponding background, text, and border classes. I will also add a unique accent dot.\n\n\n**Implementing Auto-Save**\n\nI\'m now integrating auto-save functionality with a 1-second debounce to efficiently manage local storage updates. The implementation uses `useEffect` to trigger a timeout, which saves the meeting title, attendees, transcript, and AI summary to local storage. This minimizes excessive writes.\n\n\n\n**Examining Storage Persistence**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the local storage persistence logic for meeting data. I\'ve integrated `useEffect` to ensure initial data loading from local storage, handling the retrieval of meeting title, attendees, transcript, and AI summary. I\'m also loading the meeting history, ensuring seamless state restoration on refresh. I will check for error handling on the `try...catch` blocks.\n\n\n**Verifying Package Dependencies**\n\nI\'m verifying that all framework requirements are met, and I\'m pleased to find all necessary packages like `motion` and `lucide-react` are installed, eliminating the need for additional installations. This ensures a clean and optimized setup. I\'ve considered that the page might be rendered in an iframe. To enhance the user experience, I will include a simulated mode and a browser compatibility warning, as mentioned in the PRD.\n\n\n**Adding Mock Speech**\n\nI\'m now implementing a \"Simulate Speech\" mode or \"\354\203\230\355\224\214 \353\215\260\354\235\264\355\204\260 \354\261\204\354\232\260\352\270\260\" button, providing a demo experience. I\'ll add a \"\354\213\234\353\256\254\353\240\210\354\235\264\354\205\230 \352\260\200\354\203\201 \354\235\214\354\204\261 \354\266\224\352\260\200\" button to inject a realistic corporate dialogue block in simulation mode, improving usability. I\'m drafting a simulated dialogue block list.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create `/app/page.tsx` with a beautifully designed dashboard and rich interactive states for the STT Meeting Minutes app. It includes a double-column layout with high-contrast, polished typography (Inter & JetBrains Mono), robust Speech Recognition states (with resume on interruption, silence warnings, and microphone lock displays), full LocalStorage integration (auto-saving in-progress state and robust historics list), editable timeline blocks, direct download of formats, markdown copies, and an optional server-activated Gemini AI meeting summary. I will also incorporate an interactive hotel-themed Speech Simulator so the system can be thoroughly explored and demoed instantly."
