  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing React Hooks**\n\nI\'m digging into the new React 19 ESLint rules, specifically `react-hooks/purity` and `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect`. The Next.js linter flagged `/app/applet/app/page.tsx`, and I\'m keen to dissect the issues and craft perfect fixes.\n\n\n**Refining Impure Function Handling**\n\nI\'ve tackled the impure function issue head-on. My solution involves deferred initialization of `useRef` values that depend on impure functions like `Date.now()`. I\'m replacing direct initialization with an `useEffect` hook to ensure purity during the initial render. Specifically, I\'m refactoring `useRef<number>(Date.now())` to be a `null` initial value, and updating it within a side effect. This also makes the component perfectly pure! While regular event handlers are fine with `Math.random()`, any use in the component file triggers the new lint rule.\n\n\n**Optimizing Code Purity**\n\nI\'ve got a fantastic solution to handle impure function calls. To make the component body perfectly pure and avoid linting warnings or confusion, I\'m now externalizing the ID generation function. I moved the helper function `generateId` outside the component scope, which ensures purity. The new approach uses a top-level functional expression, and I\'ve tested it against the `react-hooks/purity` rule, and it is brilliant and effective!\n\n\n"
