    thinking: "**Analyzing React 19 Bug**\n\nI\'ve hit a React 19 linter snag \342\200\223 the `react-hooks/set-state-in-effect` rule is flagging a `setState` call within my mount `useEffect`. Specifically, it\'s line 192: `if (parsed.title) setMeetingTitle(parsed.title);` that\'s causing the trouble; it seems to think I\'m doing a synchronous `setState` on mount. This triggers a warning.\n\n\n**Refining State Initialization**\n\nI\'ve realized the core problem: synchronous state updates during component mount, flagged by ESLint. The solution I\'m focusing on now is lazy state initialization using `useState(() => { ... })`. This allows me to load values from `localStorage` within the initializer function, ensuring the state is set *before* any render or effect occurs, which is much cleaner. I\'m currently designing these lazy initializers for `meetingTitle`, `attendees`, `transcript`, and `aiSummary` to implement this pattern.\n\n\n**Implementing Lazy State**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented lazy state initialization using `useState(() => { ... })` for `meetingTitle`, `attendees`, and `transcript`. This approach correctly loads values from `localStorage` within the initializer function, avoiding the synchronous update issue during component mount. I am continuing to implement this pattern for `aiSummary` too, to keep this as consistent as possible.\n\n\n"
