  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Data Syncing**\n\nOkay, I\'ve got a plan to leverage Firestore for seamless data synchronization. When a user authenticates via Google Login, I\'ll fetch and sync their meetings directly to their dedicated Firestore user space. Now, I think it\'s important to add a Firebase sync status in the codebase.\n\n\n**Refining Firebase Rules**\n\nI\'m now leaning toward storing meetings at `/users/{userId}/meetings/{meetingId}` in Firestore; it makes security rules incredibly simple. I\'ve drafted a rule that allows read and write access only if the user is signed in and the `request.auth.uid` matches the `userId`. The structure is elegant and the security is clear-cut.\n\n\n**Structuring Data Efficiently**\n\nI\'ve realized that using `/users/{userId}/meetings/{meetingId}` streamlines access because meetings are subcollections. This eliminates the need for complex `get()` operations. Transcripts fit neatly as `/users/{userId}/meetings/{meetingId}/transcript/{itemId}`, maintaining a clear relational hierarchy and strong security. I also see that `/users/{userId}/currentMeeting` supports synchronization of the current meeting, handling both logged-in and logged-out states via `localStorage`.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will inspect the `package.json` file to check existing dependencies and see if we need to install the Firebase SDK."
